> The key to this dish is using good quality Shaoxing wine and fresh ginger. The chicken is parboiled first to ensure tender meat, then braised low and slow in the wine mixture. This dish is believed to be warming and restorative in traditional Chinese medicine.

== Parboil the Chicken ==

Place @chicken{1%whole}(about 1 kg, cut into large pieces) in a #pot{} with @water{} to cover. Bring to a boil for ~{3%minutes}. 

Drain and rinse the chicken pieces under cold water to remove any impurities. Set aside.

== Prepare the Aromatics ==

Slice @fresh ginger{80%g} into thin coins or strips. Bash a few pieces lightly to release the juices.

Cut @scallions{3%stalks} into 5 cm lengths.

== Braising ==

In a #claypot{} or heavy-bottomed pot, combine the parboiled @chicken pieces{1%whole}, @ginger{80%g}, and @scallions{3%stalks}.

Add @Shaoxing wine{120%ml}, @water{300%ml}, and @salt{1%tsp}.

Bring to a boil over high heat, then reduce to a gentle simmer.

Cover and cook on low heat for ~{30%minutes}, basting the chicken occasionally with the cooking liquid.

== Finishing ==

Remove the lid and continue simmering for another ~{5-10%minutes} to reduce the sauce slightly. The sauce should be golden and fragrant, coating the chicken.

Taste and adjust seasoning with @salt{to taste} if needed.

== Serve ==

Transfer the chicken to a serving platter. Drizzle the cooking sauce over the top.

Garnish with fresh @ginger{10%g} slices and @scallions{1%stalk}(finely chopped).

Serve hot with steamed rice.